The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type | test, mask | test);
if (!alg && test)
    alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type, mask) ?
          ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD) : NULL;
```
Reason:
The first statement attempts to look up an algorithm with a modified type that includes the `CRYPTO_ALG_TESTED` flag. If the algorithm is not found (`!alg`), the second statement checks again without the test flag. If an algorithm is found but it is a "request larval" (still being initialized), it is incorrectly treated as a failed algorithm, leading to the allocation failure with the error code `ELIBBAD`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!alg && test) {
    alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type, mask);
    if (alg && !crypto_is_larval(alg)) {
        crypto_mod_put(alg);
        alg = ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD);
    }
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a check to ensure that if an algorithm is found, it verifies whether it is a "larval" algorithm using `crypto_is_larval(alg)`. If it is not larval, only then does it treat it as a failed algorithm and return `ELIBBAD`. This prevents the incorrect classification of a larval algorithm as a failed one, thus resolving the allocation failure issue.

